After the delete action was removed (see the high-severity finding above), `commentToDelete` state, `confirmDelete`, the `useDeleteComment` hook, and the `AlertDialog` block remain. None of them can be triggered. This adds cognitive load for the next reader, who will wonder how the dialog opens.

::code{file="apps/posts/src/views/comments/components/comments-list.tsx" lines="366-384"}

This finding is only relevant if the removal is kept. If the delete action is restored, the dialog becomes live again, and this finding can be closed.

Several class strings use `${cond && 'class'}`, which produces the literal text `false` in the class attribute when the condition is false. Examples are the hidden-status classes on the author button and avatar, and the body text color.

::code{file="apps/posts/src/views/comments/components/comments-list.tsx" lines="194-200"}

This is harmless in the browser, since `false` is not a real class, but it's noisy and easy to misread. The `cn` helper from `@tryghost/shade` is already used elsewhere in `apps/posts`, so using it here would be consistent.
